Li Jinhua v. Lirong Pawn Co., Ltd. — dispute over pawn contract

【Ruling Summary】

After the expiration of the pawn period (the pawn becomes "dead"), the extinction of the pawner's right of redemption based on the pawn contract neither violates legal provisions nor contravenes the practices of the pawn industry and the general understanding of the public.

Plaintiff: Li Jinhua, male, 49 years old, legal representative of Shanghai Henglongda Jewelry Company, residing at Yingxun North Road, Shanghai.

Defendant: Shanghai Lirong Pawn Co., Ltd., domiciled at Julu Road, Shanghai.

Legal representative: Shi Jiayan, Chairperson of the Board of the company.

The plaintiff, Li Jinhua, filed a lawsuit with the Shanghai Jing'an District People's Court due to a pawn dispute with the defendant, Shanghai Lirong Pawn Co., Ltd. (hereinafter referred to as Lirong Pawn Company).

The plaintiff alleged: On December 31, 2003, the plaintiff pawned a batch of K-gold jewelry to the defendant, with a pawn period of one month. After the expiration of the pawn period, the plaintiff repeatedly requested to renew the pawn, but the defendant gave verbal consent without completing the procedures. On March 12, 2004, the plaintiff, under duress from the defendant, signed a letter of commitment authorizing an auction. Subsequently, the plaintiff requested the defendant to revoke the authorization and redeem the pawned items, but was refused. The appraised value of this batch of pawned items was more than RMB 30,000. According to relevant provisions, ownership does not transfer upon expiration of the pawn period, and the pawned items do not become "debt-satisfying property" from pledged property due to the expiration. The plaintiff remains the owner of the pawned items. If the plaintiff performs the debt, the defendant should return the pawned items. The law does not grant the defendant the right to unilaterally entrust an auction for the expired pawned items. The defendant's actions infringed upon the plaintiff's rights. The plaintiff requested a judgment ordering the defendant to allow the plaintiff to redeem the pawned items.

The plaintiff submitted the following evidence:

1. A pawn ticket (customer copy) numbered 31275134, with pawn notice printed on the back, used to prove the existence of the pawn legal relationship between the parties;
2. A receipt, used to prove that the pawned items were in the possession of the defendant;
3. A letter of commitment dated March 12, 2004, used to prove that the defendant forced the plaintiff to agree to entrust Shanghai Wanrong Auction Company to auction the pawned items;
4. A notice dated March 30, 2004, used to prove that the plaintiff had explicitly requested the defendant to redeem the pawned items and informed the defendant that a lawsuit had been filed;
5. A notice dated March 30, 2004, used to prove that the plaintiff did not agree to the auction of the pawned items, sent a letter to Wanrong Auction Company requesting the revocation of the auction authorization, and agreed to bear the reasonable expenses incurred thereby.

The defendant argued: After the expiration of the pawn period, the defendant repeatedly requested the plaintiff to handle the renewal or redemption procedures. On February 29, 2004, the plaintiff issued two checks, each in the amount of RMB 37,450, to the defendant. After the defendant deposited the checks with the bank, they were returned due to "insufficient funds." The plaintiff's issuance of bad checks indicated that he was no longer capable of renewing or redeeming the pawn. According to the agreement between the parties, after the redemption period expired, the pawned items became dead pawned items. The plaintiff had no right to demand the redemption of dead pawned items, and therefore his claim should be dismissed.

The defendant submitted the following evidence:

1. A letter of commitment dated December 29, 2003, used to prove that the plaintiff agreed to pawn both his jewelry and two real estate properties to the defendant to borrow RMB 1,070,000 from the defendant;
2. A set of licenses and certificates of Shanghai Henglongda Jewelry Company (hereinafter referred to as the Jewelry Company), used to prove that the plaintiff was the legal representative of the Jewelry Company and that the pawned items in question were legally sourced;
3. A pawn ticket (stub copy) numbered 31275134, used to prove the existence of the pawn relationship between the parties and their respective rights and obligations;
4. A receipt, used to prove that the pawned items were lawfully in the possession of the defendant;
5. A pawn notice printed on the back of the pawn ticket (customer copy), used to prove that the plaintiff should have been aware of his rights and obligations;
6. A letter of commitment dated February 11, 2004, used to prove that after the expiration of the pawn period, the plaintiff promised to renew the pawn but failed to do so;
7. Two cashier's checks issued by the Jewelry Company from Shanghai Bank on February 29, 2004, used to prove that after the expiration of the pawn period, the plaintiff had agreed to pay the comprehensive fees;
8. A notice of check return from Shanghai Bank, used to prove that the plaintiff issued bad checks, indicating his lack of sincerity in renewing the pawn;
9. A letter of notification dated March 3, 2004, used to prove that the defendant had informed the plaintiff that the pawned items had become dead pawns and would be put into the auction process;
10. A letter of commitment personally signed by the plaintiff on March 12, 2004, used to prove that the plaintiff agreed to entrust Wanrong Auction Company to auction the dead pawned items and agreed to bear the relevant costs.

During the court trial, the defendant stated that it had not received the notice involved in evidence 4 submitted by the plaintiff, and that evidence 4 and 5 submitted by the plaintiff were irrelevant to this case; the plaintiff stated that the cashier's checks involved in evidence 7 and 8 submitted by the defendant were issued by the Jewelry Company and were irrelevant to the plaintiff, and that he had not received the notice involved in evidence 9 submitted by the defendant. Neither party disputed the other evidence. The court of first instance held that because the plaintiff did not prove that the notices in evidence 4 and 5 had been served on the notified persons, and evidence 5 was a notice sent to a non-party, and although the notice in evidence 9 submitted by the defendant was attached with a double-registered receipt, it was not signed for by the plaintiff himself, so it could not be proven that the plaintiff actually received it. Therefore, the above three pieces of evidence were not confirmed.

After cross-examination and authentication, the Shanghai Jing'an District People's Court confirmed the following facts:

The plaintiff, Li Jinhua, was the legal representative of the Jewelry Company, and the defendant, Lirong Pawn Company, was a limited liability company licensed to engage in pawn business.

On December 31, 2003, the plaintiff, Li Jinhua, decided to pawn a batch of K-gold jewelry to the defendant, Lirong Pawn Company. Lirong Pawn Company issued a pawn ticket numbered 31275134, stating a loan amount of RMB 405,000, with the pawn period from December 31, 2003 to January 31, 2004. The back of the customer copy of the pawn ticket was printed with "Pawn Notice," which included Article 6: "During the pawn period and within five days after the expiration of the pawn period, the pawn may be renewed with the consent of both parties. Upon renewal, the pawner shall settle the previous period's interest and current period's fees." Article 9: "After the pawn becomes dead, if the pawner and the pawnbroker agree to redeem, the overdue fees shall be negotiated by both parties." As agreed, Li Jinhua delivered the K-gold jewelry to Lirong Pawn Company for safekeeping, and Lirong Pawn Company issued a receipt to Li Jinhua and paid the agreed amount of loan to Li Jinhua.

After the expiration of the pawn period, the plaintiff, Li Jinhua, did not redeem on time, nor did he handle the renewal procedures within five days after the expiration. On February 11, 2004, Li Jinhua wrote a letter of commitment promising to pay off the current period's comprehensive fees by February 20. On February 29, 2004, the defendant, Lirong Pawn Company, received two cashier's checks numbered BN977953 and BN977955 issued by the Jewelry Company, each in the amount of RMB 37,450, with the purpose noted as "repayment." On March 2, the checks were returned to Lirong Pawn Company by Shanghai Bank due to "insufficient funds." On March 12, Li Jinhua made a written commitment stating that he "agreed to first turn the above K-gold jewelry into dead pawns and entrust Wanrong Auction Company to conduct an auction, using the auction proceeds to pay the principal of the jewelry loan, interest, fees, liquidated damages, and this auction commission, notice fee, appraisal fee, venue rental fee, and other related expenses shall be deducted from the auction proceeds in accordance with relevant regulations." On March 30, Li Jinhua filed a lawsuit demanding the redemption of the pawned items.

The focus of the dispute in this case was: After the pawn becomes dead, does the pawner have the right to demand the redemption of the pawned items?

The Shanghai Jing'an District People's Court held:

Pawn is a historically unique system of民间 financing through the transfer of possession of movables in China. After the founding of New China, the pawn industry was once banned. Although it was revived after the reform and opening-up, there was no specific legal regulation for a long time. In principle, only general legal provisions on guarantees applied to pawn relationships. On August 8, 2001, the former State Economic and Trade Commission formulated the "Measures for the Administration of Pawnbrokers," which provided in Article 3: "The term 'pawn' as used in these Measures refers to the act whereby a pawner pledges its movables or property rights as pawned property or mortgages its real estate as pawned property to a pawnbroker, pays a certain proportion of fees, obtains a loan, and pays interest on the loan, repays the loan, and redeems the pawned property within an agreed period." Article 36 provided: "After the expiration of the pawn period or the renewal period, the pawner shall redeem or renew the pawn within five days. If the pawner fails to redeem or renew within the time limit, the pawn shall become a dead pawn." Article 40 further provided for the disposal methods of dead pawned items by pawnbrokers. The "Measures for the Administration of Pawnbrokers" is an administrative regulation specifically issued by the relevant government departments of China for the pawn industry, and should be applied by reference in handling pawn disputes at present. (On February 9, 2005, the Ministry of Commerce and the Ministry of Public Security issued the "Measures for the Administration of Pawn Business," and the "Measures for the Administration of Pawnbrokers" has become invalid. — Editor's note) In addition, the pawn industry also has its own industry practices, which, to the extent they do not violate the prohibitive provisions of existing laws and regulations, should also serve as a reference in handling pawn disputes.

A pawn ticket is the pawn contract between the pawnbroker and the pawner, a written proof of the existence of the pawn relationship, and the main basis for determining the rights and obligations of both parties. Based on the facts found in this case, the signing of the pawn ticket by both parties was their true expression of intent, did not violate the prohibitive provisions of laws and administrative regulations, and therefore the pawn contract between the parties should be deemed valid. The agreements in the pawn ticket are legally binding on both parties, and both parties shall enjoy their respective rights and perform their respective obligations in accordance with the agreements in the pawn ticket. After the pawn contract takes effect, a pawn relationship is formed between the pawnbroker and the pawner. Based on the pawn contract, the pawnbroker has the right to pay the loan, take possession of the pawned property, and collect interest and fees when the pawner redeems; the pawner, while delivering the pawned property and obtaining the loan, enjoys the right of redemption over the pawned property. During the pawn period, the right of redemption is a formative right, and redemption can take legal effect merely by the unilateral expression of intent of the pawner. Redemption is the pawner's right, not an obligation; the pawnbroker cannot demand that the pawner redeem or repay the debt. According to the relevant provisions of the "Measures for the Administration of Pawnbrokers" and the agreement in the pawn ticket in this case, within five days after the expiration of the pawn period, the pawner may renew or redeem the pawn. Renewal means an extension of the pawn period, during which the pawner still enjoys the right of redemption. If the pawn is not renewed, then the fifth day after the expiration of the pawn period is the final redemption deadline agreed in the pawn contract between the parties.

Based on the evidence provided by both parties, after the expiration of the pawn period, the defendant, Lirong Pawn Company, did not refuse the plaintiff, Li Jinhua's, request for renewal. The two parties had negotiated for Li Jinhua to pay off the current period's comprehensive fees, and Lirong Pawn Company would handle the renewal procedures for Li Jinhua. Since Li Jinhua did not fulfill his promise to pay the fees, Lirong Pawn Company, in order to protect its own rights and interests, was not improper in refusing to handle the renewal procedures for Li Jinhua. The plaintiff, Li Jinhua, neither renewed nor redeemed the pawn within five days after the expiration of the pawn period. According to Article 36 of the "Measures for the Administration of Pawnbrokers" and the agreement in the pawn ticket, the pawned items in question had become dead pawns. Both parties did not dispute the fact of the dead pawn.

After the pawn becomes dead, can the pawner unilaterally demand the redemption of the pawned items again? The "Measures for the Administration of Pawnbrokers" does not explicitly provide for this. Literally, "dead" implies severance and extinction, and "dead pawn" means the severance of the pawn relationship. Once the pawn relationship is severed, the right of redemption attached to the pawn contract relationship also expires. Article 36 of the "Measures for the Administration of Pawnbrokers" provides for "dead pawns," and Article 40 further provides for the disposal methods of dead pawned items by pawnbrokers. Accordingly, it should be considered that after the pawn becomes dead, the pawner's right of redemption based on the pawn contract over the pawned items extinguishes, and the pawner cannot unilaterally demand redemption. This is an inherent meaning of "dead pawn" as referred to in the "Measures for the Administration of Pawnbrokers." Understanding the term "dead pawn" in this way also conforms to the industry practices of the pawn industry and the general understanding of the public.

Article 9 of the pawn ticket stipulates that after the pawn becomes dead, the parties may also agree to redeem. Based on the facts found in this case, if the plaintiff, Li Jinhua, had paid off the current period's comprehensive fees, the defendant, Lirong Pawn Company, would have been willing to handle the renewal procedures for Li Jinhua even after the pawn became dead. However, during the negotiation process for renewal, the cashier's checks issued by the Jewelry Company were returned by the bank due to "insufficient funds." Lirong Pawn Company believed that Li Jinhua had no sincerity in renewing the pawn and even less ability to redeem. Although Li Jinhua's agent denied in court that Li Jinhua was responsible for issuing the bad checks, since Li Jinhua was the legal representative of the Jewelry Company, the check amounts matched the fees payable based on the total loan amount received by Li Jinhua, and the checks stated the purpose as "repayment," Lirong Pawn Company had reason to believe that the Jewelry Company issued the cashier's checks to it at the direction of Li Jinhua, and that this was an act by Li Jinhua to pay the relevant fees to handle the renewal procedures. In light of these circumstances, Lirong Pawn Company believed that Li Jinhua had no sincerity or ability to redeem, and refused to agree to a redemption with Li Jinhua. Since the parties could not reach an agreement on redemption after the pawn became dead, Li Jinhua also lost the possibility of redeeming the pawned items through agreement after the dead pawn.

Although the parties in this case did not agree in advance that the pawnbroker would entrust an auction house to conduct a public auction after the pawn became dead, the plaintiff, Li Jinhua, after the pawn became dead, gave written consent to entrust Wanrong Auction Company to auction the dead pawned items. This should be regarded as an agreement reached between the parties on entrusting the auction. This agreement did not violate the relevant provisions of laws and administrative regulations. Li Jinhua claimed that this written authorization was signed under duress from the defendant, Lirong Pawn Company, but there was no relevant evidence to support this claim, so it was not recognized. Lirong Pawn Company's auction of the dead pawned items in accordance with the agreement between the parties did not constitute an infringement of Li Jinhua's rights.

Accordingly, the Shanghai Jing'an District People's Court rendered a judgment on October 25, 2004: The plaintiff Li Jinhua's claim for a judgment ordering the defendant Lirong Pawn Company to allow him to redeem the pawned items appraised at RMB 405,000 was not supported.

The plaintiff Li Jinhua was dissatisfied with the judgment of first instance and appealed to the Shanghai No. 2 Intermediate People's Court. After receiving the notice from the court of first instance urging the payment of the appeal fee, Li Jinhua failed to pay the appeal fee within the prescribed time limit. On March 23, 2005, the Shanghai No. 2 Intermediate People's Court lawfully rendered a ruling: The case was deemed as the appellant Li Jinhua having voluntarily withdrawn the appeal. The judgment of first instance has become legally effective.
